---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Apply for a Birmingham PHV Licence"
  description="How to apply for a Birmingham private hire licence: the DBS, medical, council driving test, the TTC Group knowledge test, and the application appointment."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>How to Apply for a Birmingham PHV Licence</h1>

      <p>
        This guide explains <strong>how to apply for a Birmingham private hire
        licence</strong> with Birmingham City Council, in the order the council
        sets out. The important thing to understand up front is the sequence:
        the checks and the knowledge test come <em>first</em>, and the
        application form comes last.
      </p>

      <p>
        Everything below is drawn from the council's published pages, linked at
        the bottom.
      </p>

      <h2>Check first: are new applications open?</h2>

      <p>
        Birmingham's applications page carries a notice stating that the
        council is <strong>not currently accepting new driver
        applications</strong> unless applicants have already completed a
        medical and obtained a DBS certificate through its occupational health
        service.
      </p>

      <p>
        That notice has sat on the page for some time while the newer guidance
        pages describe the full new-applicant route, so the two do not read
        consistently. Before you spend anything, email
        <a href="mailto:licensing@birmingham.gov.uk">licensing@birmingham.gov.uk</a>
        and confirm the current position for new applicants.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Read the Guide to Driver Licences</h2>

      <p>
        The council's own <strong>Guide to Driver Licences</strong> is the
        authoritative document, and it asks you to read all of it before
        contacting the office to arrange a knowledge test. It is also where the
        full document checklist lives.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Medical assessment</h2>

      <p>
        You <strong>book and pay for your medical appointment through the
        council's online form</strong>. If you need a wheelchair or dog
        exemption booking instead, email
        <a href="mailto:taximedicalanddbs@birmingham.gov.uk"
          >taximedicalanddbs@birmingham.gov.uk</a
        >
        and the team will contact you to book the appointment and take
        payment. The same address handles medical and DBS enquiries.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — DBS check</h2>

      <p>
        You must complete a <strong>conviction disclosures check (DBS)</strong>
        and/or prove previous good character by other means, as set out in the
        driver guidance. The council provides its own apply-and-pay route for
        the DBS rather than leaving you to arrange it independently.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Driving test</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>New applicants are required to take a driving test with the
        Birmingham City Council Driving School.</strong> To book, or for
        enquiries, phone <strong>0121 303 3311</strong> between 9:00am and
        12:00pm or 1:00pm and 3:00pm. You may reach an answerphone, as
        assessors are often out on jobs.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Knowledge test</h2>

      <p>
        Private hire knowledge tests are <strong>organised and conducted by TTC
        Group</strong>. TTC also offer an optional training course for
        candidates who want help preparing, and have produced a short video on
        how to prepare. You book the test, or the course, by contacting TTC
        directly by email.
      </p>

      <p>
        The paper is <strong>18 questions in three sections of 6</strong> —
        Legal, Licence Conditions and Plying for Hire — in 45 minutes, and it
        is marked by section rules rather than an overall score. Every Plying
        for Hire question must be correct. Our
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/birmingham/how-to-pass/"
          >guide to passing the Birmingham PHV test</a
        >
        explains the rules in full.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can practise for it in the <strong>PHV Prep UK</strong> app, which
        builds Birmingham papers to the same shape and marks them against the
        real section rules. It's on
        <a
          href="https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/phv-prep-uk-taxi-badge-test/id6776364932"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">the App Store</a
        >
        and
        <a
          href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.phvprepuk.app&pcampaignid=web_share"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Google Play</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 6 — Application form and appointment</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Once you have passed the knowledge test</strong>, download and
        complete the application form, then make an appointment to bring the
        form in along with the other documents listed in the driver guidance.
        General enquiries go to
        <a href="mailto:licensing@birmingham.gov.uk">licensing@birmingham.gov.uk</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What about the vehicle?</h2>

      <p>
        If you are licensing a vehicle as well, note Birmingham's age rule:
        <strong>new and replacement private hire vehicles must be younger than
        8 years</strong> from the date of first registration when the
        application is submitted. A vehicle older than eight years will not be
        licensed, and imported vehicles are assessed from their first
        registration in the country of origin where that is earlier.
      </p>

      <h2>How much does it cost?</h2>

      <p>
        Birmingham publishes a separate licensing schedule of fees and charges,
        and the medical, DBS, driving test and knowledge test are each paid
        for individually. Fees change, so we won't quote figures here — check
        the council's current schedule and confirm the TTC test fee with TTC
        before you budget.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>

      <p>
        The paperwork is the easy part — the test is the hurdle. See
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/birmingham/how-to-pass/"
          >how to pass the Birmingham PHV test</a
        >
        and the
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/birmingham/faqs/"
          >Birmingham PHV test FAQs</a
        >. Applying elsewhere in the West Midlands? We have guides for
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/solihull/how-to-apply/">Solihull</a> and
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-apply/">Wolverhampton</a> too.
      </p>

